"v":0.0},{"s":2016,"v":0.0},{"s":2017,"v":0.0},{"s":2018,"v":0.0},{"s":2019,"v":0.0},{"s":2020,"v":0.0},{"s":2021,"v":0.0},{"s":2022,"v":0.0},{"s":2023,"v":0.0},{"s":2024,"v":8.9},{"s":2025,"v":0.0}],"t_snow":{"per_decade":0.05,"lo":-0.023,"hi":0.124,"class":"noise","n":95,"first":1931,"last":2025,"intercept":-9.789630459126538,"slope":0.005039193729003359,"total":0.5},"snow_normal":0.0},"titles":{"year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows","records":"Pensacola is setting record highs far faster than chance","change":"Pensacola's nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","rain":"Pensacola has gotten wetter"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 66\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13: a typical high, but a cold night","p_yest":"The high of <strong>66\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 71\u00b0F, and the record is 81\u00b0F (1985, 2020). The low of <strong>42\u00b0F</strong> was the 28th coldest November 13 low (tied) of 148 since 1879.","t_rec":"Pensacola is setting record highs far faster than chance","p_rec":"If Pensacola's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Pensacola has set <strong>39 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 67 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">153 record highs</strong>, far more than the 67 that chance alone would give. The 1950s hold the most record highs of any decade (67). Pensacola's all-time high is 106\u00b0F, on July 14, 1980.","t_chg":"Pensacola's nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 146 complete years: Pensacola's average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.09\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.15 to \u22120.04), about \u22121.4\u00b0F since 1880. Pensacola's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.32\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.27 to +0.37), about +4.7\u00b0F since 1880. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.86\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.55 to +1.17) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.48\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.20 to +0.77). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; January and April fell. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Pensacola has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Pensacola's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.92 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.41 to +1.44), about +13.4 inches since 1880. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 68.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1953 (93.32 in); the driest, 1954 (28.52 in). Pensacola almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}